# Copy to `.env` and fill in from your Supabase project:
#   Dashboard -> Project Settings -> Data API  (URL)
#   Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API Keys  (anon / publishable key)
#
# Restart the dev server after editing — Expo inlines EXPO_PUBLIC_ vars at
# bundle time, so a running Metro will keep serving the old values.
#
# The anon key belongs in the client and ships inside the app bundle; it is a
# publishable key, and row level security is what actually protects the data.
# The *service role* key bypasses RLS entirely — it must never go in this file
# or anywhere else under src/.
#
# Leave both blank and the app runs local-only, exactly as it did before the
# database existed.

EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# The Anthropic API key does NOT belong here. It is a server-side secret used
# only by the Edge Functions, and every EXPO_PUBLIC_ value is compiled into the
# app bundle where anyone can read it. Set it on the server instead:
#
#   npx supabase secrets set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
#
# Neither does CRON_SECRET, which gates `coach-cadence`:
#
#   npx supabase secrets set CRON_SECRET=$(openssl rand -base64 32)
#
# The service role key does not belong in *any* file in this repo, gitignored
# or not. It bypasses every RLS policy — it can read and write every user's
# rows across all accounts — and this directory is the one place where a rename
# to EXPO_PUBLIC_ silently ships it to every device. The only thing that needs
# it is `scripts/seed-test-data.ts`, which takes it from the environment for a
# single command:
#
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY='...' \
#     npx tsx --env-file=.env.local scripts/seed-test-data.ts
